LLAMA-3 PROMPT DEBUG
input_only: False
instruction: 'The Even Feed Foot- sometimes referred to as the walking foot- is designed with a set of feed dogs which allow the fabric to feed without shirting and eliminates the problem of the under layer of fabric coming up short. The even feed foot is also great for topstitching- which can be spoilt due to irregularity of stitching. This is often caused by the bulk created with the seam allowance especially around collars and reveres. Using the walking foot for topstitching will eliminate this problem and give perfect stitching every time. Based on the passage above, Can you explain the benefits of using an even feed foot when sewing?'
target: 'The even feed foot allows the fabric to feed evenly without shifting, eliminating the problem of the under layer of fabric coming up short. It is also great for topstitching, preventing irregularity of stitching due to bulk created with seam allowance, especially around collars and reveres. Using the even feed foot for topstitching results in perfect stitching every time.'
formatted_prompt: '<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nThe Even Feed Foot- sometimes referred to as the walking foot- is designed with a set of feed dogs which allow the fabric to feed without shirting and eliminates the problem of the under layer of fabric coming up short. The even feed foot is also great for topstitching- which can be spoilt due to irregularity of stitching. This is often caused by the bulk created with the seam allowance especially around collars and reveres. Using the walking foot for topstitching will eliminate this problem and give perfect stitching every time. Based on the passage above, Can you explain the benefits of using an even feed foot when sewing?<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThe even feed foot allows the fabric to feed evenly without shifting, eliminating the problem of the under layer of fabric coming up short. It is also great for topstitching, preventing irregularity of stitching due to bulk created with seam allowance, especially around collars and reveres. Using the even feed foot for topstitching results in perfect stitching every time.<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you please tell me which types of sewing projects would benefit the most from using an even feed foot?<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nSewing projects that involve multiple layers of fabric, slippery or stretchy fabrics, quilting, and sewing projects that require topstitching would benefit the most from using an even feed foot. Examples of sewing projects that would benefit from using an even feed foot include garments with collars or reveres, upholstery, curtains or drapes, and bags or purses.'
