instruction: 'Given the text: Calf-Path - This poem was written by Sam Walter Foss (a "long" time ago) - I carried it with me for 45 years (so far) and bought the domain name immidiately it was available. To me it has meant a lot! Please enjoy!\nCan you paraphrase the meaning of Calf-Path and its significance to the author?'
target: 'The poem Calf-Path was written a long time ago by Sam Walter Foss and the author has carried it with them for 45 years. The author finds the poem meaningful and even bought the domain name for it as soon as it became available. The significance of the poem is not specifically stated, but it is clear that the author has a deep connection to it and hopes others will enjoy it as well.'
formatted_prompt: '<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nGiven the text: Calf-Path - This poem was written by Sam Walter Foss (a "long" time ago) - I carried it with me for 45 years (so far) and bought the domain name immidiately it was available. To me it has meant a lot! Please enjoy!\nCan you paraphrase the meaning of Calf-Path and its significance to the author?<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThe poem Calf-Path was written a long time ago by Sam Walter Foss and the author has carried it with them for 45 years. The author finds the poem meaningful and even bought the domain name for it as soon as it became available. The significance of the poem is not specifically stated, but it is clear that the author has a deep connection to it and hopes others will enjoy it as well.<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you recite the poem for me?<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nYes, here is the full text of the poem "Calf-Path" by Sam Walter Foss:\n\nOne day, through the primeval wood,\nA calf walked home, as good calves should;\nBut made a trail all bent askew,\nA crooked trail, as all calves do.\n\nSince then three hundred years have fled,\nAnd, I infer, the calf is dead.\nBut still he left behind his trail,\nAnd thereby hangs my moral tale.\n\nThe trail was taken up next day\nBy a lone dog that passed that way;\nAnd then a wise bell-wether sheep\nPursued the trail o\'er vale and steep,\nAnd drew the flock behind him too,\nAs good bell-wethers always do.\n\nAnd from that day, o\'er hill and glade,\nThrough those old woods a path was made,\nAnd many men wound in and out,\nAnd dodged, and turned, and bent about,\nAnd uttered words of righteous wrath\nBecause \'twas such a crooked path.\n\nBut still they followed — do not laugh -\nThe first migrations of that calf,\nAnd through this winding wood-way stalked\nBecause he wobbled when he walked.\n\nThis forest path became a lane,\nThat bent, and turned, and turned again;\nThis crooked lane became a road,\nWhere many a poor horse with his load\nToiled on beneath the burning sun,\nAnd traveled some three miles in one.\n\nAnd thus a century and a half\nThey trod the footsteps of that calf.\nThe years passed on in swiftness fleet,\nThe road became a village street,\nAnd this, before men were aware,\nA city\'s crowded thoroughfare.\n\nAnd soon the central street was this\nOf a renowned metropolis;\nAnd men two centuries and a half\nTrod in the footsteps of that calf.\n\nEach day a hundred thousand rout\nFollowed the zigzag calf about;\nAnd o\'er his crooked journey went\nThe traffic of a continent.\n\nA hundred thousand men were led\nBy one calf near three centuries dead.\nThey followed still his crooked way,\nAnd lost one hundred years a day;\nFor thus such reverence is lent\nTo well-established precedent.\n\nA moral lesson this might teach\nWere I ordained and called to preach;\nFor men are prone to go it blind\nAlong the calf-paths of the mind,\nAnd work away from sun to sun\nTo do what other men have done.\n\nThey follow in the beaten track,\nAnd out and in, and forth and back,\nAnd still their devious course pursue,\nTo keep the path that others knew.\n\nThey keep the path a sacred groove,\nAlong which all their lives they move;\nBut how the wise old wood-gods laugh,\nWho saw the first primeval calf!\n\nAh, many things this tale might teach —\nBut I am not ordained to preach.<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you provide some examples of how people follow "calf-paths of the mind" in today\'s society?<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCertainly! Here are a few examples of how people may follow "calf-paths of the mind" in contemporary society:\n\n1. Career Choices: Many people choose their professions based on their upbringing, societal expectations, or what they see others doing. Rather than exploring their passions and what will make them happy, they follow the path laid out for them by others, ultimately leading to dissatisfaction.\n\n2. Social Media Use: People may follow trends and what others are doing on social media without considering if it aligns with their values or priorities. This can lead to a lack of authenticity and uniqueness in their online presence.\n\n3. Religious beliefs: Many people may follow the religious beliefs of their parents or community without questioning or exploring their own beliefs. This can result in a lack of personal growth and self-awareness.\n\n4. Politics: People may blindly follow a political party or ideology without researching or understanding their own beliefs and values, leading to a lack of critical thinking and independent opinion.\n\n5. Personal habits: People may follow routines and habits because they\'ve always done it that way, without considering if it is beneficial or necessary. This can lead to missed opportunities for growth and change.<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you provide more insights into the moral of the poem "Calf-Path" and how it applies to our lives?<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCertainly! The moral of the poem "Calf-Path" is that we should not blindly follow the paths of those who came before us, simply because it\'s the way things have always been done. The poem encourages us to break free from traditional paths and explore our own paths, even if it means going against the grain.\n\nThe poem\'s message of individualism and critical thinking applies to all aspects of our lives. We should challenge societal expectations and norms in order to find our own paths and purpose. In doing so, we create a unique and fulfilling life for ourselves that is not dictated by others.\n\nThe poem\'s message also emphasizes the importance of questioning tradition and not being afraid to create our own path. By doing this, we can not only find our own purpose and happiness but also make a positive impact on society by pushing boundaries and bringing innovative ideas to the forefront.\n\nIn summary, the poem "Calf-Path" serves as a reminder that we should not simply follow the paths that others have travelled before us. Instead, we should carve out our own paths and live a life that is truly our own. By doing so, we can find fulfillment, purpose, and make a positive impact on the world around us.'
